WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.200
<v Narrator>Mistakes are where psychological safety is tested hardest.

2
00:00:03.350 --> 00:00:07.750
Picture a service message that goes out with the wrong date.

3
00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:11.500
The readings say to keep two needs in view.

4
00:00:11.650 --> 00:00:13.250
First, the present problem.

5
00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:19.000
The date may need correcting right away, by the person responsible for the message.

6
00:00:19.150 --> 00:00:20.750
Second, the learning question.

7
00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:26.500
How did the wrong date get in, and how did it get past review?

8
00:00:26.650 --> 00:00:32.250
Calling the employee careless can hide a confusing source or a missed review step.

9
00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:38.000
Saying mistakes never matter ignores the effect on the people who read the message.

10
00:00:38.150 --> 00:00:42.950
A thoughtful response handles the current problem first, then asks a specific

11
00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:44.300
learning question.

12
00:00:45.050 --> 00:00:50.650
<v Narrator>In another practice story, a team member sends a review package without its source

13
00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:52.000
attachment.

14
00:00:52.150 --> 00:00:56.150
The receiver cannot check a condition and sends it back.

15
00:00:56.300 --> 00:01:02.300
The team member offers to talk about the mistake at the next team learning meeting.

16
00:01:02.450 --> 00:01:06.850
Reflection may help, but it does not supply the missing attachment.

17
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:09.800
Start with what the work needs now.

18
00:01:09.950 --> 00:01:12.350
Then look at how it happened.

19
00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:17.700
In the story, the package checklist covered the draft but not its source.

20
00:01:17.850 --> 00:01:22.250
The sender also assumed the receiver could find the source alone.

21
00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:26.400
A specific fix might add the source to the checklist.

22
00:01:26.550 --> 00:01:32.150
A promise never to make a mistake again offers certainty that nobody can deliver.

23
00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:36.900
<v Narrator>Under pressure, teams often reach for one of two shortcuts.

24
00:01:37.050 --> 00:01:38.650
One is public blame.

25
00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:41.200
Name the person and move on.

26
00:01:41.350 --> 00:01:43.750
That can produce a quick explanation.

27
00:01:43.900 --> 00:01:49.900
It also discourages people from disclosing errors, and it can hide a gap in the

28
00:01:50.050 --> 00:01:51.250
process.

29
00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:56.600
The other shortcut is to declare that nobody is responsible, because the system

30
00:01:56.750 --> 00:01:57.950
caused it.

31
00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:01.300
That can make disclosure easier for a moment.

32
00:02:01.450 --> 00:02:07.850
But it goes beyond a leader's authority, and it can leave the next task with no

33
00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:09.200
owner.

34
00:02:09.350 --> 00:02:12.950
The readings describe a third path: learning with boundaries.

35
00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:14.300
Correct the work.

36
00:02:14.450 --> 00:02:16.450
Examine conditions and choices together.

37
00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:21.800
Keep any formal matter, such as a personnel question, in its own route.

38
00:02:22.550 --> 00:02:24.950
<v Narrator>Some mistakes are not about documents.

39
00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:27.900
They are about how someone was treated.

40
00:02:28.050 --> 00:02:30.050
One reading puts it directly.

41
00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:36.200
When a comment or decision lands badly, the person with more power leads the repair.

42
00:02:36.350 --> 00:02:39.150
The leader names it and takes responsibility.

43
00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:44.900
The person harmed should not have to explain, educate or forgive on a schedule.

44
00:02:45.050 --> 00:02:50.250
Most awkward moments come from not knowing, and the fitting response is information

45
00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:52.400
and a chance to change.

46
00:02:52.550 --> 00:02:56.150
A lack of knowledge does not erase harm, though.

47
00:02:56.300 --> 00:03:00.700
And it does not decide whether a formal reporting process applies.

48
00:03:00.850 --> 00:03:03.650
Intentional harm goes to the formal channels.

49
00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:07.600
<v Narrator>Here is a practice case from the readings.

50
00:03:07.750 --> 00:03:13.750
A supervisor of a policy team dismisses a newer analyst's concern that a notice will

51
00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:15.100
confuse families.

52
00:03:15.250 --> 00:03:18.850
There is no time to revisit it, she says.

53
00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:23.000
Two weeks later, a county partner raises the same problem.

54
00:03:23.150 --> 00:03:27.150
The supervisor notices the analyst has stopped speaking in meetings.

55
00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:28.500
What now?

56
00:03:28.650 --> 00:03:33.850
Telling him privately to speak up more puts the burden back on him.

57
00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:39.600
Waiting for him to raise it puts repair on the person with less power.

58
00:03:39.750 --> 00:03:45.350
The reading's stronger response: at the next team meeting, she says he raised this

59
00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:51.500
first, that she should have listened, what will change in the notice, and how she

60
00:03:51.650 --> 00:03:54.050
will handle concerns from now on.

61
00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:58.600
That works because his concern was raised in an open meeting.

62
00:03:58.750 --> 00:04:04.750
If someone raised a concern privately, ask them before you credit them in front of

63
00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:06.100
others.

64
00:04:06.850 --> 00:04:11.650
<v Narrator>You do not have to be a supervisor to repair a response.

65
00:04:11.800 --> 00:04:16.200
If you brushed off a colleague's question, you can say so.

66
00:04:16.350 --> 00:04:19.150
“My reply cut off a relevant question.

67
00:04:19.300 --> 00:04:25.700
Here is how it will get an answer now.” That is more useful than asking the

68
00:04:25.850 --> 00:04:28.650
colleague to confirm that everything is fine.

69
00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:33.200
An apology can matter without settling what the other person experienced.

70
00:04:33.350 --> 00:04:36.150
Also check where your first reading traveled.

71
00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:42.700
Did it end up in a note, a summary, or someone's view of a colleague's ability?

72
00:04:42.850 --> 00:04:48.450
A private change of mind may not fix a label that has already moved.

73
00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:51.400
Correct what you can, within your role.

